    "summary": "Acoustic set dazzles, electric half burns—Lovelight, Good Lovin', Ripple shine",
    "blurb": "The August‑19 ’70 Fillmore West show delivers a flawless acoustic opening and a high‑energy electric second set. Highlights include Jerry’s searing solos on “Good Lovin'” and “Truckin’”, Pigpen’s piano on “How Long Blues”, and the soaring “Lovelight”/“Turn On Your Lovelight”. Overall the performance is strong despite a few minor tuning slips.",
    "review": "On August 19, 1970 at Fillmore West the Dead rolled out an acoustic set that many fans still cite as one of the finest early‑’70 outings. Jerry’s lead work was “tip‑of‑the‑hand” blues on “How Long Blues”, while Pigpen’s piano added a “scattershot honky‑tonk” charm to numbers like “Candyman” and “Ripple”. The transition into the electric half kept the momentum high; “Good Lovin'” erupted into a fiery double‑take, “Truckin’” featured blistering pedal‑steel licks, and the rare “Turn On Your Lovelight” (often shortened to “Lovelight”) became an electrifying centerpiece. Phil’s bass was surprisingly clear for an audience tape, laying down a lively foundation that let both drummers—Mickey and Bill—drive the groove with solid, propulsive rhythms. Though a few tuning hiccups slipped in during the electric set, the overall pacing feels natural and engaging, making this show a must‑listen for fans of the band’s early acoustic/electric duality.",
